<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sri Jayabupati</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. <a href="/years/1030">1030</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Jayabupati</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sri Jayabupati was a king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-strait">Sunda</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> in the early eleventh century, known from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanghyang-tapak-inscription">Sanghyang Tapak inscriptions</a> of 1030 near Sukabumi. In them he declared a section of river sacred and forbade fishing there under threat of curses. The inscriptions, written in a style close to that of eastern Java, are among the earliest dated sources naming a Sundanese ruler.</p></div>
